  data:  # de1a
    kind: invariant  # de1a
    title: "smart-test selects nothing for a change confined to tests/fixtures/** — a fixture edit changes a gate's inputs without running the gate"  # de1a
    status: violated  # de1a
    priority: 2  # de1a
    parent:  # de1a
    tags:  # de1a
    - dogfood  # de1a
    - infra  # de1a
    isbefore: []  # de1a
    duplicate_of: []  # de1a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # de1a
    pr_ref:  # de1a
    description: "**Mechanic**: `scripts/smart-test` selects tests by an import-dependency reverse slice over Python modules, plus the WI-jozan top-level map for `scripts/*` and `.agent/hooks/_shared/*`. Neither mechanism can see a **test-data fixture**: a change confined to `packages/*/tests/fixtures/**` (or `tests/fixtures/**`) touches no importable module and matches no source-to-test mapping rule, so it contributes nothing to the selection set. The script contains zero occurrences of the string \"fixture\" across 1045 lines.\n\n**Expectation**: Editing a fixture selects the test files that read that fixture, so a fixture change cannot alter a gate's inputs without the gate running.\n\n**Scope**: Measured live while shipping the WI-duguk container-member parity columns. That change edited seven files under `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/fixtures/emission-parity/` plus one test module. The generated manifest recorded `Changed files: 12`, `Changed source files: 0`, `Selected tests: 1` — only `test_emission_parity_matrix.py`, which was selected because the test module itself changed, NOT because the fixtures did. The corpus has a **second** consumer, `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_entrypoint_parity.py` (same `CORPUS` constant), which was not selected. Had the added enum/interface constructs broken entrypoint detection for any of the eight fixture languages, per-PR CI would have reported green. Verified by hand instead: 8 passed.\n\n**Exposure**: Every fixture corpus with more than one consuming test. `emission-parity` is the measured instance (2 consumers). The pattern generalizes to `route-parity` and `schema-coverage-corpus`, and to any future shared corpus — the more reusable a fixture set is, the more consumers a single edit can silently invalidate.\n\n**Consequence**: A fixture edit is a change to a gate's *inputs*. When it selects no consuming test, the PR reports green on a gate that never re-ran against the data it now uses — the same \"a test that cannot be selected is indistinguishable from a test that passes\" failure the smart-test selection work has now hit five distinct ways (misnamed test file; 1:1 top-level map; changed-test pattern excluding root `tests/`; that check living inside a fires-only-when-nothing-else-matched fallback; and now fixture-data changes selecting nothing). The twice-daily `full-suite` job does run the whole tree, so the exposure is a latency gap of up to ~12h rather than a permanent blind spot — but the per-PR gate is the one whose green a merge decision is made on.\n\n**Candidate remedy (unverified, and note that a filed remedy decays faster than its symptom — re-measure before building)**: map a changed path under a `fixtures/` directory to the test modules that reference that directory, resolved by grepping test sources for the fixture directory name rather than by import graph. A cheaper approximation is to map any change under `<pkg>/tests/fixtures/<name>/` to every test module in `<pkg>/tests/` that mentions `<name>`. Both need a positive control proving the new rule selects a test that the current rule does not."  # de1a
    fields:  # de1a
      statement: "Editing a test fixture selects every test module that reads that fixture, so a change to a gate's input data cannot land without the gate re-running against it."  # de1a
      root_cause: "smart-test resolves tests by an import-dependency reverse slice over Python modules plus the WI-jozan top-level source-to-test map. A fixture is neither an importable module nor a mapped source path, so it contributes nothing to the selection set; the script contains zero occurrences of the string fixture across 1045 lines."  # de1a
- op: discuss  # cf0e
  at: '2026-07-31T06:21:36Z'  # cf0e
  by: agent  # cf0e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cf0e
  clock: 2  # cf0e
  nonce: cf0e  # cf0e
  message: "SECOND MEASURED INSTANCE, distinct mechanism, same consequence -- the manifest under-selects and CI measures the wrong thing.\n\nAn UNTRACKED new test file is invisible to smart-test. scripts/smart-test:562-567 unions committed + staged + unstaged changes, all three via `git diff`, which never reports untracked files. So a brand-new test module contributes nothing to selection until it is staged or committed.\n\nMeasured live while draining swift for WI-duguk. The new packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/tests/test_swift_container_member_symbols.py was the SOLE coverer of the new protocol-requirement branch in swift.py. Unstaged, it was absent from the manifest while three sibling swift test files were present, and the scoped-coverage gate reported swift.py at 99% with 8 missing lines (725-726, 744-752) -- precisely the lines that file's own passing test exercises. `git add` of the test file, then re-run, is the fix; the same run then reports the branch covered.\n\nWhy this is worth recording separately from the fixture hole in this item's description: the fixture hole is about a file KIND the selector cannot map (data, not an importable module), whereas this is about a file STATE the selector cannot see (untracked). The two need different remedies, and neither is subsumed by the other. What they share is the failure signature: the run is green, the count is plausible, and the thing you just wrote never ran.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO L43: the existing lesson records the outcome (\"a new standalone test that is the sole coverer of a changed file can be dropped from the committed manifest\") and prescribes check-package-coverage before pushing. It does not name the untracked-file cause, which is the part that makes it predictable rather than something to be caught by luck. The mitigation that actually worked both times was reading the coverage error rather than the green summary line.\n\nFRAMING QUESTION FOR THE HUMAN, not resolved here: this is now the sixth distinct route to \"a test that cannot be selected is indistinguishable from a test that passes\" (misnamed test file; 1:1 top-level map; changed-test pattern excluding root tests; that check inside a fires-only-when-nothing-matched fallback; fixture data; untracked files). At six, the item type may be wrong -- the class is arguably \"smart-test's changed-file enumeration and its test-resolution rules each have holes, and nothing measures their coverage\" rather than a list of individual holes. That is a scope call, not an agent call."  # cf0e
